{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'support.title'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{#
	**Reaching whoever runs this installation** (XIV-123, §8.17).

	The customer's end of a pair. [XIV-120] put the operator's announcements on
	this person's dashboard; this is the way back, and until it existed there was
	no channel from a customer to an operator at all.

	**Everything on this page comes out of two databases and says which.** The
	question is a row in the customer's own database, because §4.4 gives their
	instance no write privilege anywhere in the control plane; the status and the
	reply are rows in the control plane, read directly, because `SELECT` on the
	registry is what that grant has always allowed. So the answer appears the
	instant an operator writes it, and the *question* waits for the next
	collection — which is the asymmetry this page states out loud rather than
	papering over.

	**No attachments, no screenshots, no priority.** An ERP support queue is not a
	helpdesk product, and each of those is a step towards being one. A priority in
	particular is a promise about ordering that this installation has no way to
	keep.
#}
{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<h1 class="h3 mb-1">{{ 'support.title'|trans }}</h1>
		<p class="text-body-secondary">{{ 'support.intro'|trans }}</p>

		<div class="row g-4">
			<div class="col-12 col-lg-5">
				<form method="post" class="card">
					<div class="card-body">
						<h2 class="h6">{{ 'support.raise'|trans }}</h2>
						<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('support') }}">

						<div class="mb-3">
							<label class="form-label" for="support-subject">{{ 'support.subject'|trans }}</label>
							<input class="form-control" id="support-subject" name="subject" required
							       maxlength="{{ max_subject }}">
						</div>

						<div class="mb-3">
							<label class="form-label" for="support-body">{{ 'support.body'|trans }}</label>
							<textarea class="form-control" id="support-body" name="body" rows="8" required
							          maxlength="{{ max_body }}"></textarea>
							{#
								What to write, said before somebody writes the wrong thing. An
								operator reading "it does not work" has to ask two questions
								before they can start, and the round trip costs a collection
								interval each way.
							#}
							<div class="form-text">{{ 'support.body_hint'|trans }}</div>
						</div>

						<button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">{{ 'support.send'|trans }}</button>

						{#
							**The delay, on the screen of the person it happens to.** A ticket
							is written into this installation's own database and an operator
							sees it at the next collection; saying so here is the difference
							between a product that is quiet for ten minutes and one that looks
							broken for ten minutes. §8.17 refuses to name an interval, because
							the crontab that decides it belongs to whoever runs this
							installation and a number printed here would be this repository
							guessing at somebody else's file.
						#}
						<p class="form-text mb-0 mt-3">{{ 'support.delay'|trans }}</p>
					</div>
				</form>
			</div>

			<div class="col-12 col-lg-7">
				{% if tickets is empty %}
					<div class="card">
						<div class="card-body">
							<h2 class="h6">{{ 'support.none'|trans }}</h2>
							<p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ 'support.none_hint'|trans }}</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				{% else %}
					{#
						The company's tickets rather than this reader's. A colleague who
						asked the same question on Tuesday should find the answer instead of
						asking it again, which is most of what a screen buys over an email
						— and the name of whoever raised each one is on the row, so nothing
						here is anonymous. It is simply not private between colleagues.
					#}
					{% for ticket in tickets %}
						<article class="card mb-3 {{ ticket.isOutstanding ? '' : 'opacity-75' }}">
							<div class="card-body">
								<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-start gap-3">
									<h2 class="h6 mb-1">{{ ticket.subject }}</h2>
									{#
										Colour and the word, never colour alone — the tenant list's
										rule, and here it carries the honest fourth case: a ticket
										nobody has collected yet has no status at all, and says so
										rather than borrowing "open". That is §8.11's *absence says
										it exactly*, in the direction the customer is looking.
									#}
									{% if not ticket.isReceived %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ 'support.not_received'|trans }}</span>
									{% elseif ticket.status.value == 'closed' %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-success">{{ ticket.status.label|trans }}</span>
									{% elseif ticket.status.value == 'in_progress' %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-info">{{ ticket.status.label|trans }}</span>
									{% else %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-warning">{{ ticket.status.label|trans }}</span>
									{% endif %}
								</div>

								<p class="small text-body-secondary mb-2">
									{{- 'support.raised_by'|trans({
										'%name%': ticket.raisedByLabel,
										'%time%': ticket.raisedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i'),
									}) -}}
								</p>

								{# Plain text rendered as plain text, with the line breaks somebody typed. #}
								<p class="mb-0" style="white-space: pre-wrap">{{ ticket.body }}</p>

								{% if ticket.reply %}
									{#
										The operator's answer, drawn as a distinct block rather than
										as another paragraph: the one thing a person opening this
										page is looking for is whether anybody has said anything.
									#}
									<div class="mt-3 p-3 rounded bg-body-secondary">
										<div class="small text-body-secondary mb-1">
											{{- 'support.replied_by'|trans({
												'%name%': ticket.replyAuthorLabel,
												'%time%': ticket.repliedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i'),
											}) -}}
										</div>
										<div style="white-space: pre-wrap">{{ ticket.reply }}</div>
									</div>
								{% endif %}
							</div>
						</article>
					{% endfor %}
				{% endif %}
			</div>
		</div>
	</main>
{% endblock %}
